 Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract. 1 Introduction 2 Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity 2.1 A sign-normalization and what it counts at negative integers 2.2 Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity 2.3 Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma 2.4 Two basic operations and their matrices 2.5 Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth 3 Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins 3.1 Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point 3.2 Exponential growth for every non-integer q > 0 q>0 4 The planar evaluation spectrum for q > 2 q>2 4.1 Attainable vectors 4.2 Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth 5 A planar ping–pong for q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) ∖ { 1 } q\in(0,2)\setminus\{1\} 6 Concluding remarks References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2512.19600v1 [math.CO] 22 Dec 2025 Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra Rafael Miyazaki , Cosmin Pohoata and Michael Zheng Address: Department of Mathematics, Emory University, Atlanta, GA, 30322, USA Email address: {rafael.kazuhiro.miyazaki, cosmin.pohoata, xzhe226}@emory.edu Abstract. Around 10 years ago, Agol and Krushkal showed that the number of chromatic polynomials P G P_{G} arising from graphs G G on n n vertices grows exponentially with n n , by establishing that the (dual) flow polynomial F G ​ ( 3 + 5 2 ) F_{G}\left(\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right) already takes on exponentially many values, if one varies G G over all planar cubic graphs G G on n n vertices. We show, more generally, that the size of the set { P G ​ ( q ) : | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n } \{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\} is exponential in n n , for every fixed real number q ≠ 0 , 1 , 2 q\neq 0,1,2 . In fact, our approach can also be pushed to show that P G ​ ( q ) P_{G}(q) already takes on exponentially many values, if we only vary G G over all planar graphs on n n vertices. The case q = 3 q=3 confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the NP -completeness of planar 3 3 -colorability. 1. Introduction A proper q q –coloring of a finite undirected graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) is an assignment φ : V → [ q ] \varphi:V\to[q] such that adjacent vertices receive different colors. When q ∈ ℤ ≥ 1 q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} , the number of proper q q –colorings is denoted P G ​ ( q ) P_{G}(q) , and it is a classical theorem of Birkhoff and Whitney that there is a unique polynomial P G ​ ( x ) ∈ ℤ ​ [ x ] P_{G}(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x] which encodes the number of proper q q -colorings of G G when evaluated at x = q x=q . This is the so-called chromatic polynomial of graph G G , a polynomial of degree | V | |V| and with leading coefficient equal to 1 1 . This object was first introduced by Birkhoff [ 5 ] in 1912 as part of an algebraic approach for the Four Color Problem. Birkhoff’s original motivation was to study colorings of planar maps by encoding them in a polynomial and analyzing its behavior at small integer values, particularly at x = 4 x=4 . Although this approach did not yield a proof of the Four Color Theorem, it initiated a rich line of research and established the chromatic polynomial as a fundamental graph invariant. Whitney [ 15 ] subsequently extended Birkhoff’s ideas to general graphs and introduced many of the structural properties of chromatic polynomials that are now standard. Among these is the deletion–contraction identity: for any edge e e of G G , (1) P G ​ ( x ) = P G − e ​ ( x ) − P G / e ​ ( x ) , P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x), where G − e G-e denotes deletion and G / e G/e denotes contraction. This identity places chromatic polynomials within the broader framework of the Tutte polynomial [ 13 ] . The chromatic polynomial encodes substantial information about G G . For instance, the chromatic number satisfies χ ⁡ ( G ) = min ⁡ { q ∈ ℤ ≥ 1 : P G ​ ( q ) > 0 } . \chi(G)=\min\{q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}:P_{G}(q)>0\}. Evaluations at negative integers also have a rich combinatorial meaning: Stanley [ 10 ] showed that | P G ​ ( − 1 ) | = # ⁡ { acyclic orientations of ​ G } , |P_{G}(-1)|=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\}, and more generally his chromatic reciprocity theorem interprets ( − 1 ) | V | ​ P G ​ ( − m ) (-1)^{|V|}P_{G}(-m) , for m ∈ ℤ ≥ 1 m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} , as a weighted count of acyclic orientations together with order-preserving maps to [ m ] [m] . Chromatic polynomial spectrum. In [ 1 ] , Agol and Krushkal proved that the number of chromatic polynomials of planar triangulations on n n vertices grows exponentially with n n . In a few words, their argument works with duals of planar cubic graphs and considers the number of distinct values taken by the flow polynomial at the special value Q = ( 3 − 5 ) / 2 Q=(3-\sqrt{5})/2 (for a planar graph G G , its flow polynomial is precisely P G ∗ ​ ( x ) P_{G^{*}}(x) , where G ∗ G^{*} is the dual of G G ). At this particular parameter Q Q , the chromatic algebra (a certain algebra over ℂ ⁡ [ Q ] \mathbb{C}[Q] formed from linear combinations of isotopy classes of planar graphs in a rectangle with a fixed number of univalent points on top and bottom) satisfies an additional local relation (coming from Temperley–Lieb / Jones–Wenzl theory), which collapses a natural boundary module from dimension three to dimension two. Two explicit local operations on planar graphs G G then act on P G ∗ ​ ( Q ) P_{G^{*}}(Q) by 2 × 2 2\times 2 matrices whose squares generate a free semigroup, yielding exponentially many distinct evaluations and hence exponentially many distinct chromatic polynomials. See [ 2 , 1 ] for more details and further references. In this paper we study the asymptotics of the evaluation spectra of the chromatic polynomial. First, let 𝒢 n \mG_{n} be the set of all simple graphs on n n vertices. For each fixed real number q q and integer n ≥ 1 n\geq 1 , we can now define 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) ≔ { P G ​ ( q ) : G ∈ 𝒢 n } . \mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}\,\}. In words, 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}(q) represents the set of distinct values attained by P G ​ ( q ) P_{G}(q) as G G ranges over all simple graphs on n n vertices. Furthermore, let 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) ≔ { P G ​ ( q ) : G ∈ 𝒢 n pl } \mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\,\} where 𝒢 n pl \mG_{n}^{\pl} is the set of all n n –vertex, simple, planar graphs. Trivially, | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≥ | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(q)\right| . Our results. We establish lower bounds for 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}(q) and 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q) for arbitrary fixed q q . For integer q ≥ 3 q\geq 3 the value P G ​ ( q ) P_{G}(q) counts proper q q –colorings, and Agol conjectured that the spectrum 𝖲 n pl ​ ( 3 ) \mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(3) should also grows exponentially over 𝒢 n pl \mG_{n}^{\pl} ; see [ 12 ] for more context, in particular a motivation coming from the NP -completeness of 3 3 –colorability of planar graphs. Our main theorem confirms this conjecture and, more generally, shows that the evaluation spectrum is exponential in n n for every fixed q ∉ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\notin\{0,1,2\} . Theorem 1.1 . For every real number q ∉ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\notin\{0,1,2\} , the evaluation spectrum | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)| grows exponentially in n n . Moreover, we can show the following uniform lower bounds: | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ { 2 ( n − 2 ) / 2 , q < 0 , 2 n / 8 − o ⁡ ( n ) , 0 < q < 2 , F n − 2 = Θ ⁡ ( φ ( n − 2 ) / 2 ) , q > 2 , |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\begin{cases}2^{(n-2)/2},&q<0,\\[4.0pt] 2^{n/8-o(n)},&\hskip-11.38109pt0<q<2,\\[4.0pt] \sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{(n-2)/2}),&q>2,\end{cases} where ( F n ) n (F_{n})_{n} is the Fibonacci sequence and φ = ( 1 + 5 ) / 2 \varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2 is the golden ratio. In particular, Theorem 1.1 and the trivial inequality | 𝒫 n | ≥ | 𝖲 n ​ ( − 1 ) | ≥ | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( − 1 ) | |\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(-1)\right| together imply that | 𝒫 n | ≥ 2 ( n − 2 ) / 2 |\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq 2^{(n-2)/2} . This fact also improves quantitatively upon the lower bound from [ 2 ] . Furthermore, the lower bounds from Theorem 1.1 hold true even if we additionally require the family of (planar) graphs in 𝒢 n pl \mG_{n}^{\pl} to be connected. See Remark 2.11 for more details. Before we say more about the proof of Theorem 1.1 , let us first remark that for q ∈ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\in\{0,1,2\} the spectrum is not exponential even if we allow G G to vary over all graphs on n n vertices. Indeed, • P G ​ ( 0 ) = 0 P_{G}(0)=0 for every graph with at least one vertex, so | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( 0 ) | = | 𝖲 n ​ ( 0 ) | = 1 |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(0)|=|\mathsf{S}_{n}(0)|=1 ; • P G ​ ( 1 ) = 1 P_{G}(1)=1 if G G is edgeless and P G ​ ( 1 ) = 0 P_{G}(1)=0 otherwise, so | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( 1 ) | = 1 |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(1)|=1 and | 𝖲 n ​ ( 1 ) | = 2 |\mathsf{S}_{n}(1)|=2 ; • P G ​ ( 2 ) P_{G}(2) counts proper 2 2 –colorings, so it is 0 0 for non-bipartite G G , and equals 2 c ⁡ ( G ) 2^{c(G)} for bipartite G G with c ⁡ ( G ) c(G) connected components. Hence | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( 2 ) | ≤ | 𝖲 n ​ ( 2 ) | = n + 1 |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(2)|\leq|\mathsf{S}_{n}(2)|=n+1 . The proof of Theorem 1.1 is more delicate when q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) q\in(0,2) (and precise quantitative bounds are also more difficult to extract), so, as a prequel to that side of the story, we will first use an elementary argument to show that | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)| is exponential in that range, by reducing the problem to the case when q < 0 q<0 . We record this as a second theorem, in greater generality. Theorem 1.2 . For every non-integer real number q > 0 q>0 , and n ≥ ⌈ q ⌉ + 2 n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2 , | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 ( n − ⌈ q ⌉ − 2 ) / 2 . |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-\lceil q\rceil-2)/2}. In particular, if q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) q\in(0,2) , then Theorem 1.2 immediately implies that | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 ( n − 4 ) / 2 |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\geq 2^{(n-4)/2} for all n ≥ 4 n\geq 4 . Last but not least, let us also emphasize that for positive integers q ∉ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\} , exponential growth for | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right| is best possible, up to the correct base of the exponent. Indeed, if q ∈ ℤ ≥ 1 q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} then every n n –vertex graph has at most q n q^{n} proper q q –colorings, so | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≤ q n + 1 |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1 . When q q is not a nonnegative integer, there is however no comparable a priori bound of the form | P G ​ ( q ) | ≤ C n |P_{G}(q)|\leq C^{n} . When q = − 1 q=-1 , Stanley’s theorem [ 10 ] gives | P K n ​ ( − 1 ) | = n ! |P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n! , and, more generally, it is not difficult to show that for any fixed q ∈ ℝ ∖ ℤ ≥ 0 q\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} the complete graph satisfies P K n ( q ) = ( q ) n = q ( q − 1 ) ⋯ ( q − n + 1 ) P_{K_{n}}(q)=(q)_{n}=q(q-1)\cdots(q-n+1) , whose magnitude grows on the order of n ! n! . These factorial-scale values suggest that, at least for some non-integer or negative points, the spectrum itself should be superexponential . We will discuss this some more in Section 6 . Related work and proof ideas. A closely related spectrum problem was recently resolved for the spanning-tree enumerator. Let τ ⁡ ( G ) \tau(G) be the number of spanning trees of G G , and set 𝒯 ⁡ ( n ) ≔ { τ ⁡ ( G ) : G ∈ 𝒢 n } , \mathcal{T}(n)\coloneqq\{\tau(G):G\in\mG_{n}\}, as well as 𝒯 pl ​ ( n ) ≔ { P G ​ ( q ) : G ∈ 𝒢 n pl } . \mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)\coloneqq\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\}. In [ 8 ] , Chan, Kontorovich and Pak proved that | 𝒯 pl ​ ( n ) | |\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)| grows exponentially in n n , via a connection to continued fractions, thin orbits, and a deep result of Bourgain-Kontorovich [ 6 ] on the so-called Zaremba conjecture [ 16 ] . See [ 8 ] and the references therein for more information. Soon after, Alon–Bucić–Gishboliner [ 3 ] gave a much simpler alternative proof, which also came with improved quantitative bounds and results for other classes of graphs. Both arguments made crucial use of the deletion–contraction formula for the spanning-tree enumerator τ \tau and a handful of basic operations that increase the number of vertices of the graph, and which allow one to track the evolution of the parameters τ ⁡ ( G − e ) \tau(G-e) and τ ⁡ ( G / e ) \tau(G/e) (for various edges e e of G G ), as the graph G G undergoes these changes. In [ 3 ] , however, Alon, Bucić and Gishboliner noted that the parameters τ ⁡ ( G − e ) \tau(G-e) and τ ⁡ ( G / e ) \tau(G/e) of G G can sometimes instantly uniquely decode the entire process, an observation which drastically simplified the analysis. For the chromatic polynomial spectrum, Agol and Krushkal [ 1 ] also previously employed a similar strategy to show that | 𝒫 n | |\mathcal{P}_{n}| grows exponentially with n n . An important difference in their argument is that the basic operations employed are less simple than the ones in [ 3 ] or [ 8 ] , and so one cannot readily decode the process if their operations are used freely to construct their n n –vertex graphs. In order to circumvent this barrier, they consider only graphs obtained through a sequence of block operations, each of which combines some of their set of basic operations. To decode the sequence of block operations from the final parameters obtained, Agol and Krushkal then appeal to a simple application of the ping–pong lemma, a basic tool from geometric group theory. See for example [ 9 ] and the references therein or [ 7 ] . Our argument, in some sense, unifies the two strategies from [ 1 ] and [ 3 ] . To prove Theorem 1.1 , we make use of block operations that consist of sequences of two of the basic operations used in [ 3 ] , and then establish various ping–pong lemma type criteria, in order to decode. Organization of the paper. The proof of Theorem 1.1 is most pleasant when q < 0 q<0 , so we set the stage by discussing this case first in Section 2 . After that, in Section 3 , we take a first dip into the more problematic case when q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) q\in(0,2) , which we will first address for the | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)| . Using a different kind of combinatorial argument, we will derive a lower bound by reducing the problem to the case when q ∈ ( − 1 , 0 ) q\in(-1,0) , for which the result from Section 1.1 applies. This argument is the content of Theorem 1.2 and works in greater generality for every non-integer real number q > 0 q>0 , and n ≥ ⌈ q ⌉ + 2 n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2 . It does not apply directly to lower bound | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)| but it provides a better quantitative bound for | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)| than Theorem 1.1 does in this range. In Section 4 , we continue the proof of Theorem 1.1 with a more complicated ping–pong argument showing that | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)| is exponential in n n for each q > 2 q>2 . In Section 5 , we then complete the proof of Theorem 1.1 by showing that | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n}(q)| is exponential in n n for every q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) ∖ { 1 } q\in(0,2)\setminus\{1\} as well, by using a more intricate combination of block constructions. In Section 6 , we end with some concluding remarks and a couple of natural open problems. Acknowledgments The last two authors are also grateful to Matija Bucić for an excellent talk on [ 3 ] at the IAS/PCMI summer program on extremal combinatorics, which inspired the present paper. C.P. was supported by NSF grant DMS-2246659. 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity Throughout this section we fix a real number q < 0 q<0 and write λ ≔ − q > 0 \lambda\coloneqq-q>0 . 2.1. A sign-normalization and what it counts at negative integers Define the sign-normalized evaluation (2) Z G ​ ( λ ) ≔ ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G ​ ( − λ ) = ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G ​ ( q ) . Z_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(q). For a fixed n n , multiplying by the constant factor ( − 1 ) n (-1)^{n} is injective, so bounding the number of distinct values of Z G ​ ( λ ) Z_{G}(\lambda) over | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n |V(G)|=n is equivalent to bounding | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)| . Proposition 2.1 (Chromatic reciprocity [ 10 , Thm. 1.2] ) . Let G G be a loopless graph on n n vertices and let m ∈ ℤ ≥ 1 m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} . Then Z G ​ ( m ) = ( − 1 ) n ​ P G ​ ( − m ) Z_{G}(m)=(-1)^{n}P_{G}(-m) equals the number of pairs ( 𝒪 , f ) (\mathcal{O},f) where 𝒪 \mathcal{O} is an acyclic orientation of G G and f : V ⁡ ( G ) → [ m ] f:V(G)\to[m] is order-preserving with respect to 𝒪 \mathcal{O} (i.e., if u → v u\to v is an oriented edge of 𝒪 \mathcal{O} , then f ⁡ ( u ) ≤ f ⁡ ( v ) f(u)\leq f(v) ). In particular, Z G ​ ( 1 ) = # ⁡ { acyclic orientations of ​ G } Z_{G}(1)=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\} . Remark 2.2 . For non-integer λ > 0 \lambda>0 there is, in general, no literal set being counted by Z G ​ ( λ ) Z_{G}(\lambda) ; rather Z G ​ ( λ ) Z_{G}(\lambda) is the polynomial extension of the counting function in Proposition 2.1 . Our proof below uses only the structural consequences that persist for all λ > 0 \lambda>0 : positivity and an additive deletion–contraction recurrence. (For a geometric perspective on these reciprocity phenomena, see [ 4 ] .) 2.2. Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity A standard consequence of Whitney’s broken-circuit theorem (see, e.g., [ 11 ] ) is that the coefficients of P G ​ ( x ) P_{G}(x) alternate in sign: for a loopless graph G G on n n vertices there exist integers a 0 , … , a n ≥ 0 a_{0},\dots,a_{n}\geq 0 such that (3) P G ​ ( x ) = ∑ k = 0 n ( − 1 ) k ​ a k ​ x n − k . P_{G}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k}a_{k}x^{n-k}. Lemma 2.3 (Positivity for λ > 0 \lambda>0 ) . If G G is loopless and λ > 0 \lambda>0 , then Z G ​ ( λ ) > 0 Z_{G}(\lambda)>0 . Proof. Substituting x = − λ x=-\lambda into ( 3 ) gives ( − 1 ) n ​ P G ​ ( − λ ) = ∑ k = 0 n a k ​ λ n − k , (-1)^{n}P_{G}(-\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}\lambda^{n-k}, which is strictly positive since a 0 = 1 a_{0}=1 . ∎ Lemma 2.4 (Additive deletion–contraction for Z Z ) . Let e e be a non-loop edge of G G . Then (4) Z G ​ ( λ ) = Z G − e ​ ( λ ) + Z G / e ​ ( λ ) . Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G-e}(\lambda)+Z_{G/e}(\lambda). Proof. From deletion–contraction P G ​ ( x ) = P G − e ​ ( x ) − P G / e ​ ( x ) P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x) and the definition ( 2 ) we compute Z G ​ ( λ ) = ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G ​ ( − λ ) = ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G − e ​ ( − λ ) − ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G / e ​ ( − λ ) . Z_{G}(\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G-e}(-\lambda)-(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda). The first term is Z G − e ​ ( λ ) Z_{G-e}(\lambda) , which has the same number of vertices, while ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ P G / e ​ ( − λ ) = − ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G / e ) | ​ P G / e ​ ( − λ ) = − Z G / e ​ ( λ ) , (-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-(-1)^{|V(G/e)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-Z_{G/e}(\lambda), giving ( 4 ). ∎ 2.3. Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma Definition 2.5 (Feasible vectors) . Let e e be an edge of a planar graph G G . Define the feasible vector witnessed by ( G , e ) (G,e) to be v λ ​ ( G , e ) ≔ ( Z G / e ​ ( λ ) Z G − e ​ ( λ ) ) . v_{\lambda}(G,e)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}Z_{G/e}(\lambda)\\ Z_{G-e}(\lambda)\end{pmatrix}. We write v ⁡ ( G , e ) = v λ ​ ( G , e ) v(G,e)=v_{\lambda}(G,e) when λ \lambda is clear from context. We call v v an n n –feasible vector if there exists an n n –vertex planar graph G G and an edge e ∈ E ⁡ ( G ) e\in E(G) such that v ⁡ ( G , e ) = v v(G,e)=v . We record a simple yet crucial lemma connecting the number of such vectors with size of 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q) . Lemma 2.6 (Square-root lemma) . If there are at least N N distinct n n –feasible vectors, then | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ N . |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\sqrt{N}. Lemma 2.6 is completely analogous to [ 3 , Lemma 2.1] . Proof. Let ( t i , u i ) (t_{i},u_{i}) be N N distinct n n –feasible vectors with witnesses ( G i , e i ) (G_{i},e_{i}) . Consider the multiset of second coordinates { u i } \{u_{i}\} . If at least N \sqrt{N} of the u i u_{i} are distinct, then, since edge-deletion preserves planarity, the values Z G i − e i ​ ( λ ) = u i Z_{G_{i}-e_{i}}(\lambda)=u_{i} give at least N \sqrt{N} distinct evaluations coming from n n –vertex planar graphs. Otherwise, some value u u occurs for at least N \sqrt{N} indices. For those indices the pairs ( t i , u ) (t_{i},u) are distinct, hence the sums t i + u t_{i}+u are distinct. By Lemma 2.4 , t i + u = Z G i ​ ( λ ) t_{i}+u=Z_{G_{i}}(\lambda) , so again we obtain at least N \sqrt{N} distinct values. Finally P G ​ ( q ) = ( − 1 ) n ​ Z G ​ ( λ ) P_{G}(q)=(-1)^{n}Z_{G}(\lambda) for | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n |V(G)|=n , so the same lower bound holds for | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)| . ∎ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices We use two operations on witnessed edges. Let e = a ​ b e=ab be an edge of G G . Definition 2.7 (Basic operations) . (S) ( Subdivision ) Subdivide e e by a new vertex w w , replacing e e with the path a − w − b a\!-\!w\!-\!b . Let G ′ G^{\prime} be the new graph and set S ⁡ ( G , e ) ≔ ( G ′ , a ​ w ) S(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\prime},aw) . (B) ( Add a vertex adjacent to both endpoints ) Add a new vertex w w adjacent to both a a and b b . Let G ∗ G^{\ast} be the new graph and set B ⁡ ( G , e ) ≔ ( G ∗ , e ) B(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\ast},e) . We repeatedly use the standard leaf identity: if H H is obtained from G G by attaching a new leaf to an existing vertex, then (5) P H ​ ( x ) = ( x − 1 ) ​ P G ​ ( x ) and hence Z H ​ ( λ ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ Z G ​ ( λ ) . P_{H}(x)=(x-1)P_{G}(x)\qquad\text{and hence}\qquad Z_{H}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G}(\lambda). Lemma 2.8 (Matrix action) . Let e e be an edge of a planar graph G G , and write v ⁡ ( G , e ) = ( t u ) v(G,e)=\binom{t}{u} . Then ( v ∘ S ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( 1 1 0 λ + 1 ) ​ ( t u ) , ( v ∘ B ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( λ + 1 0 1 λ + 2 ) ​ ( t u ) . (v\circ S)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\ 0&\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u},\qquad(v\circ B)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda+1&0\\ 1&\lambda+2\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u}. Proof. Let e = a ​ b e=ab . Operation S S . Let ( G ′ , f ) = S ⁡ ( G , e ) (G^{\prime},f)=S(G,e) with f = a ​ w f=aw . Contracting f f merges a a and w w and collapses the path a − w − b a\!-\!w\!-\!b back to the single edge a ​ b ab , so ( G ′ / f ) ≅ G (G^{\prime}/f)\cong G and hence Z G ′ / f ​ ( λ ) = Z G ​ ( λ ) = Z G / e ​ ( λ ) + Z G − e ​ ( λ ) = t + u Z_{G^{\prime}/f}(\lambda)=Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+u by Lemma 2.4 . Deleting f f makes w w a leaf attached to b b in the graph G − e G-e , so by ( 5 ) Z G ′ − f ​ ( λ ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ Z G − e ​ ( λ ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ u . Z_{G^{\prime}-f}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)u. This gives the stated matrix for S S . Operation B B . Let ( G ∗ , e ) = B ⁡ ( G , e ) (G^{\ast},e)=B(G,e) with new vertex w w adjacent to both a a and b b . Contracting e e in G ∗ G^{\ast} merges a a and b b ; the two edges a ​ w aw and b ​ w bw become parallel edges from the merged vertex to w w , which do not affect proper colorings, so G ∗ / e G^{\ast}/e is obtained from G / e G/e by attaching a leaf. Hence by ( 5 ), Z G ∗ / e ​ ( λ ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ Z G / e ​ ( λ ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ t . Z_{G^{\ast}/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)t. For deletion, set H ≔ G ∗ − e H\coloneqq G^{\ast}-e . We claim the polynomial identity (6) P H ​ ( x ) = P G / e ​ ( x ) + ( x − 2 ) ​ P G − e ​ ( x ) . P_{H}(x)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x). Indeed, apply deletion–contraction to the edge a ​ w aw in H H . Deleting a ​ w aw leaves a leaf at b b attached to G − e G-e , hence P H − a ​ w ​ ( x ) = ( x − 1 ) ​ P G − e ​ ( x ) P_{H-aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x) . Contracting a ​ w aw identifies a a with w w and turns b ​ w bw into an edge a ​ b ab , recovering G G (up to harmless parallel edges), so P H / a ​ w ​ ( x ) = P G ​ ( x ) = P G − e ​ ( x ) − P G / e ​ ( x ) P_{H/aw}(x)=P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x) . Thus, P H ​ ( x ) P_{H}(x) equals P H − a ​ w ​ ( x ) − P H / a ​ w ​ ( x ) = ( x − 1 ) ​ P G − e ​ ( x ) − ( P G − e ​ ( x ) − P G / e ​ ( x ) ) = P G / e ​ ( x ) + ( x − 2 ) ​ P G − e ​ ( x ) , P_{H-aw}(x)-P_{H/aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x)-\bigl(P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)\bigr)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x), proving ( 6 ). Evaluating ( 6 ) at x = − λ x=-\lambda and multiplying by ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( H ) | = ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | + 1 (-1)^{|V(H)|}=(-1)^{|V(G)|+1} yields Z H ​ ( λ ) = Z G / e ​ ( λ ) + ( λ + 2 ) ​ Z G − e ​ ( λ ) = t + ( λ + 2 ) ​ u . Z_{H}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+(\lambda+2)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+(\lambda+2)u. This gives the stated matrix for B B . ∎ In view of this lemma, we will sometimes write, say, S S for the matrix corresponding to the operation S S . 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth For v = ( x y ) v=\binom{x}{y} with y ≠ 0 y\neq 0 , define r ⁡ ( v ) ≔ x / y r(v)\coloneqq x/y . We also define the feasible vector witnessed after a sequence of operations as follows. Definition 2.9 . Let a = ( a 1 , … , a t ) a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{t}) be a word in the semigroup { B , S } ∗ \{B,S\}^{\ast} , G G be a planar graph, and e e be an edge of G G . Then define 𝐯 ⁡ ( a , G , e ) ≔ ( v ∘ a t ∘ a t − 1 ∘ ⋯ ∘ a 1 ) ​ ( G , e ) , \mathbf{v}(a,G,e)\coloneqq(v\circ a_{t}\circ a_{t-1}\circ\dots\circ a_{1})(G,e), the feasible vector witnessed by the pair generated through the sequence of operations encoded by a a starting with the pair ( G , e ) (G,e) . We can now prove the following lemma, which guarantees that different words of the same length generate different feasible vectors if starting from an appropriate pair ( G , e ) (G,e) . Lemma 2.10 . Let v 0 ≔ v ⁡ ( K 2 , e ) v_{0}\coloneqq v(K_{2},e) where e e is the unique edge of K 2 K_{2} . Then v 0 = ( λ λ 2 ) v_{0}=\binom{\lambda}{\lambda^{2}} and r ⁡ ( v 0 ) = 1 / λ r(v_{0})=1/\lambda . Moreover, on ratios the operations act by f S ​ ( r ) = r + 1 λ + 1 , f B ​ ( r ) = ( λ + 1 ) ​ r r + λ + 2 . f_{S}(r)=\frac{r+1}{\lambda+1},\qquad f_{B}(r)=\frac{(\lambda+1)r}{r+\lambda+2}. Let I ≔ ( 0 , 1 / λ ] I\coloneqq(0,1/\lambda] . Then f S ​ ( I ) ⊂ ( 1 / ( λ + 1 ) , 1 / λ ] f_{S}(I)\subset\bigl(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda\bigr] and f B ​ ( I ) ⊂ ( 0 , 1 / ( λ + 1 ) ] f_{B}(I)\subset\bigl(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)\bigr] , so the images are disjoint and contained in I I . Consequently, if w ≠ w ′ w\neq w^{\prime} are words in the semigroup { S , B } ∗ \{S,B\}^{\ast} with the same number of letters, then r ⁡ ( 𝐯 ⁡ ( w , K 2 , e ) ) ≠ r ⁡ ( 𝐯 ⁡ ( w ′ , K 2 , e ) ) r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))\neq r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e)) . Proof. The base computation is immediate: K 2 / e K_{2}/e is a single vertex graph, so Z K 2 / e ​ ( λ ) = λ Z_{K_{2}/e}(\lambda)=\lambda ; K 2 − e K_{2}-e has two isolated vertices, so Z K 2 − e ​ ( λ ) = λ 2 Z_{K_{2}-e}(\lambda)=\lambda^{2} . From Lemma 2.8 , if v = ( x y ) v=\binom{x}{y} then S ​ v = ( x + y ( λ + 1 ) ​ y ) , B ​ v = ( ( λ + 1 ) ​ x x + ( λ + 2 ) ​ y ) , Sv=\binom{x+y}{(\lambda+1)y},\qquad Bv=\binom{(\lambda+1)x}{x+(\lambda+2)y}, so the ratio maps are f S , f B f_{S},f_{B} . Now fix r ∈ I r\in I , so 0 < r ≤ 1 / λ 0<r\leq 1/\lambda . Since f S f_{S} is strictly increasing, f S ​ ( r ) > f ⁡ ( 0 ) = 1 λ + 1 and f S ​ ( r ) ≤ f S ​ ( 1 / λ ) = 1 λ . f_{S}(r)>f(0)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}\quad\text{and}\quad f_{S}(r)\leq f_{S}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda}. Thus f S ​ ( I ) ⊂ ( 1 / ( λ + 1 ) , 1 / λ ] f_{S}(I)\subset(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda] . Similarly, since f B f_{B} is strictly increasing, f B ​ ( r ) > f B ​ ( 0 ) = 0 and f B ​ ( r ) ≤ f B ​ ( 1 / λ ) = 1 λ + 1 . f_{B}(r)>f_{B}(0)=0\quad\text{and}\quad f_{B}(r)\leq f_{B}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}. Thus f B ​ ( I ) ⊂ ( 0 , 1 / ( λ + 1 ) ] f_{B}(I)\subset(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)] . Hence, the images are disjoint. Finally, suppose r ⁡ ( 𝐯 ⁡ ( w , K 2 , e ) ) = r ⁡ ( 𝐯 ⁡ ( w ′ , K 2 , e ) ) r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))=r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e)) . Observe that they must both lie in I I . By the image disjointness just proved and the fact that they have the same number of letters, they are either both empty or the last letter of w w must equal the last letter of w ′ w^{\prime} . In the former case, w = w ′ w=w^{\prime} , while in the latter case, since both matrices in Lemma 2.8 are invertible, we can cancel the last letter in each word and iterate to conclude that w = w ′ w=w^{\prime} . ∎ Remark 2.11 . Note that the operations defined in Definition 2.7 preserve graph planarity and connectivity, and increase the number of vertices of the graph by one. In fact, if we apply a sequence of these operations starting with ( K 3 , e ) (K_{3},e) or ( K 4 , e ) (K_{4},e) , e e being an arbitrary edge of the respective graph, then it is not hard to inductively show that the resulting graph is 2 2 –connected. Indeed, this follows from Whitney’s theorem [ 14 ] , since the graph will always have an ear decomposition: after applying S S , replace e e by a − w − b a-w-b within the ears containing e e ; after applying B B , we simply gain a new ear. In particular, replacing the starting graph in Lemma 2.10 by ( K 3 , e ) (K_{3},e) , Lemma 2.10 , and the subsequent Lemma 5.3 and Lemma 4.5 , all yield 2 2 –connected, planar witnesses, so after applying Lemma 2.6 and Lemma 4.2 respectively, we get the same bound asymptotically on the evaluation spectrum even if we require G ∈ 𝒢 n pl G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl} to be connected. We can now prove the first bound of Theorem 1.1 . Theorem 2.12 . Fix a real number q < 0 q<0 . Then for every n ≥ 2 n\geq 2 , | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 ( n − 2 ) / 2 . |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-2)/2}. Proof. Starting from ( K 2 , e ) (K_{2},e) on 2 2 vertices, each application of S S or B B adds exactly one vertex and preserves planarity. Thus every word w ∈ { S , B } n − 2 w\in\{S,B\}^{n-2} yields an n n –vertex witness and an n n –feasible vector 𝐯 ⁡ ( w , K 2 , e ) \mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e) . By Lemma 2.10 , these 2 n − 2 2^{n-2} vectors are all distinct. Applying Lemma 2.6 finishes the proof: | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 n − 2 = 2 ( n − 2 ) / 2 . ∎ |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\geq\sqrt{2^{n-2}}=2^{(n-2)/2}.\qed Remark 2.13 . It is not a coincidence that Theorem 2.12 recovers the same quantitative bound from [ 3 , Corollary 2.6] . Our two basic operations S S and B B are two out of the three operations used in [ 3 ] , and our ping–pong Lemma 2.10 morally substitutes their [ 3 , Theorem 2.5] in the analysis. It is perhaps important to add that the third operation used in [ 3 ] to subsequently upgrade their lower bound does not behave well for our purposes. Namely, in the notation of Definition 2.5 and 2.7 , if we consider the witness ( G ∗ , w ​ a ) (G^{\ast},wa) , then one can check that v ⁡ ( G ∗ , w ​ a ) = ( 1 1 λ + 1 λ + 1 ) ​ v ​ ( G , e ) . v(G^{\ast},wa)=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\ \lambda+1&\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}v(G,e). The matrix is singular, so it is not possible to use this operation and still get a free semigroup. 3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins In this section we show that once the evaluation spectrum 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}(q) grows exponentially for every point in ( − 1 , 0 ) (-1,0) , it is automatically exponentially large for every non-integer positive point. In particular, this handles the problematic range q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) q\in(0,2) . The reduction uses the join operation and a simple coloring-counting identity. 3.1. Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point Definition 3.1 (Join) . For graphs G G and H H on disjoint vertex sets, the join G ∨ H G\vee H is obtained from G ⊔ H G\sqcup H by adding all edges between V ⁡ ( G ) V(G) and V ⁡ ( H ) V(H) . Lemma 3.2 . Let G G be a graph and let m ≥ 1 m\geq 1 . Then (7) P G ∨ K m ​ ( x ) = ( x ) m ​ P G ​ ( x − m ) , P_{G\vee K_{m}}(x)=(x)_{m}\,P_{G}(x-m), where ( x ) m ≔ x ( x − 1 ) ⋯ ( x − m + 1 ) (x)_{m}\coloneqq x(x-1)\cdots(x-m+1) is the falling factorial. Proof. Fix an integer x ≥ m x\geq m . In a proper x x -coloring of G ∨ K m G\vee K_{m} , the m m vertices of the clique K m K_{m} must receive m m distinct colors, and none of those colors may appear on G G (since every vertex of G G is adjacent to every vertex of K m K_{m} ). There are ( x ) m (x)_{m} ways to color K m K_{m} , and then P G ​ ( x − m ) P_{G}(x-m) ways to color G G using the remaining x − m x-m colors. Thus ( 7 ) holds for all integers x ≥ m x\geq m , and hence as an identity of polynomials. ∎ Corollary 3.3 . Fix a real q q and an integer m ≥ 1 m\geq 1 such that ( q ) m ≠ 0 (q)_{m}\neq 0 . Then for every n ≥ 1 n\geq 1 , | 𝖲 n + m ​ ( q ) | ≥ | 𝖲 n ​ ( q − m ) | . |\mathsf{S}_{n+m}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q-m)|. In particular, for N ≥ m N\geq m one has | 𝖲 N ​ ( q ) | ≥ | 𝖲 N − m ​ ( q − m ) | |\mathsf{S}_{N}(q)|\geq|\mathsf{S}_{N-m}(q-m)| . Proof. Define Φ ⁡ ( G ) ≔ G ∨ K m \Phi(G)\coloneqq G\vee K_{m} , which maps n n –vertex graphs to ( n + m ) (n+m) -vertex graphs. By Lemma 3.2 , P Φ ⁡ ( G ) ​ ( q ) = P G ∨ K m ​ ( q ) = ( q ) m ​ P G ​ ( q − m ) . P_{\Phi(G)}(q)=P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q)=(q)_{m}\,P_{G}(q-m). Since ( q ) m ≠ 0 (q)_{m}\neq 0 , the map y ↦ ( q ) m ​ y y\mapsto(q)_{m}y is injective on real values, so distinct values of P G ​ ( q − m ) P_{G}(q-m) yield distinct values of P G ∨ K m ​ ( q ) P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q) . ∎ 3.2. Exponential growth for every non-integer q > 0 q>0 We can then prove the following lower bound for the chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra at non-integer positive numbers. Theorem 3.4 . Let q > 0 q>0 be a non-integer real and set m ≔ ⌈ q ⌉ m\coloneqq\lceil q\rceil . Then q − m ∈ ( − 1 , 0 ) q-m\in(-1,0) and ( q ) m ≠ 0 (q)_{m}\neq 0 , and for all n ≥ m + 2 n\geq m+2 , | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 ( n − m − 2 ) / 2 . |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}. Proof. Since q q is not an integer, none of the factors q , q − 1 , … , q − m + 1 q,q-1,\dots,q-m+1 vanishes, hence ( q ) m ≠ 0 (q)_{m}\neq 0 . Also m − 1 < q < m m-1<q<m implies q − m ∈ ( − 1 , 0 ) q-m\in(-1,0) . Apply Corollary 3.3 with this m m and use Theorem 2.12 at the negative point q − m q-m : for n ≥ m + 2 n\geq m+2 we have n − m ≥ 2 n-m\geq 2 and hence | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≥ | 𝖲 n − m ​ ( q − m ) | ≥ | 𝖲 n − m pl ​ ( q − m ) | ≥ 2 ( n − m − 2 ) / 2 . ∎ |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}.\qed Remark 3.5 . The trick to adjoin a clique discussed in this section works well to show Theorem 3.4 , but it does not allow us to control certain properties of the resulting graph. In fact, if one applies the operation B B twice or more within a sequence of operations, then the resulting graph contains a K 2 , 3 K_{2,3} -subdivision, so joining by just one vertex destroys planarity. 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for q > 2 q>2 In this section, we prove the last bound of Theorem 1.2 for 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}(q) by showing the following Theorem 4.1 . Let q > 2 q>2 be a real number and n ≥ 3 n\geq 3 , then | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ F n − 2 = Θ ⁡ ( φ n / 2 ) , \left|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{n/2}), where ( F n ) n (F_{n})_{n} is the Fibonacci sequence and φ = ( 1 + 5 ) / 2 \varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2 is the golden ratio. In particular, we get the bound for integers q ≥ 3 q\geq 3 . 4.1. Attainable vectors Let G G be a planar graph and e e one of its edges. Define the attainable vector witnessed by ( G , e ) (G,e) as w q ​ ( G , e ) ≔ ( P G / e ​ ( q ) P G − e ​ ( q ) ) . w_{q}{(G,e)}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}P_{G/e}(q)\\[2.0pt] P_{G-e}(q)\end{pmatrix}. We write w ⁡ ( G , e ) = w q ​ ( G , e ) w(G,e)=w_{q}(G,e) when q q is clear from context. We call w w an n n –attainable vector if there exists an n n –vertex planar graph G G and a edge e e of G G such that w = w ⁡ ( G , e ) w=w(G,e) . We remark that (8) w q ​ ( G , e ) = ( P G / e ​ ( q ) P G − e ​ ( q ) ) = ( ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | − 1 ​ Z G / e ​ ( − q ) ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ​ Z G − e ​ ( − q ) ) = ( ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | − 1 0 0 ( − 1 ) | V ⁡ ( G ) | ) ​ v − q ​ ( G , e ) , w_{q}(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}P_{G/e}(q)\\[2.0pt] P_{G-e}(q)\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}(-1)^{|V(G)|-1}Z_{G/e}(-q)\\ (-1)^{|V(G)|}Z_{G-e}(-q)\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}(-1)^{|V(G)|-1}&0\\ 0&(-1)^{|V(G)|}\end{pmatrix}v_{-q}(G,e), where v − q ​ ( G , e ) v_{-q}(G,e) is the feasible vector witnessed by ( G , e ) (G,e) with parameter λ = − q \lambda=-q . In what follows, let A n = ( ( − 1 ) n − 1 0 0 ( − 1 ) n ) . A_{n}=\begin{pmatrix}(-1)^{n-1}&0\\ 0&(-1)^{n}\end{pmatrix}. With this remark in hand, we can show a couple of lemmas analogous to those found in Section 2 . Lemma 4.2 (Square-root lemma) . If there are at least N N distinct n n –attainable vectors, then | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ N . |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\sqrt{N}. Proof. Since A n A_{n} is invertible, there are at least N N distinct n n -feasible vectors. By Lemma 2.6 , | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ N . |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\geq\sqrt{N}. ∎ The same basic operations B B and S S defined in Section 2 will be the building blocks of our construction of exponentially many n n –attainable vectors. Lemma 4.3 (Matrix action) . Let e e be an edge of a planar graph G G , and write w ⁡ ( G , e ) = ( t u ) w(G,e)=\binom{t}{u} . Then ( w ∘ S ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( − 1 1 0 q − 1 ) ​ ( t u ) , ( w ∘ B ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( q − 1 0 1 q − 2 ) ​ ( t u ) . (w\circ S)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\ 0&q-1\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u},\qquad(w\circ B)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}q-1&0\\ 1&q-2\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u}. Proof. Let n = | V ⁡ ( G ) | n=|V(G)| and observe that Lemma 2.8 and ( 8 ) imply ( w ∘ S ) ​ ( G , e ) = A n + 1 ​ ( 1 1 0 − q + 1 ) ​ A n − 1 ​ ( t u ) = ( − 1 1 0 q − 1 ) ​ ( t u ) (w\circ S)(G,e)=A_{n+1}\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\ 0&-q+1\end{pmatrix}A_{n}^{-1}\binom{t}{u}=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\ 0&q-1\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u} and ( w ∘ B ) ​ ( G , e ) = A n + 1 ​ ( − q + 1 0 1 − q + 2 ) ​ A n − 1 ​ ( t u ) = ( q − 1 0 1 q − 2 ) ​ ( t u ) . (w\circ B)(G,e)=A_{n+1}\begin{pmatrix}-q+1&0\\ 1&-q+2\end{pmatrix}A_{n}^{-1}\binom{t}{u}=\begin{pmatrix}q-1&0\\ 1&q-2\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u}. ∎ As before, we will interchangeably write, say, S S for the operation and the corresponding matrix. 4.2. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth In order to prove the case where q ≥ 3 q\geq 3 is an integer in Theorem 1.2 , we will follow a similar strategy to the negative evaluation case. We choose an initial graph G 0 = K 3 G_{0}=K_{3} , and show that operations B B and D = S 2 D=S^{2} , the concatenation of two consecutive S S operations, act on attainable vector ratios with disjoint images. A simple calculation then shows that there exist exponentially many n n –attainable vectors. Recall that for v = ( x y ) v=\binom{x}{y} with y ≠ 0 y\neq 0 , we have defined r ⁡ ( v ) ≔ x / y r(v)\coloneqq x/y . The following definition is a direct analogy of Definition 2.9 . Definition 4.4 . Let a = ( a 1 , … , a t ) a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{t}) be a word in the semigroup { B , D } ∗ \{B,D\}^{\ast} , G G be a planar graph, and e e be an edge of G G . Then define 𝐰 ⁡ ( a , G , e ) ≔ ( w ∘ a t ∘ a t − 1 ∘ ⋯ ∘ a 1 ) ​ ( G , e ) , \mathbf{w}(a,G,e)\coloneqq(w\circ a_{t}\circ a_{t-1}\circ\dots\circ a_{1})(G,e), the attainable vector witnessed by the pair generated through the sequence of operations encoded by a a starting with the pair ( G , e ) (G,e) . We can now prove a lemma analogous to Lemma 2.10 . Lemma 4.5 . Let q > 2 q>2 and let w 0 ≔ w ⁡ ( K 3 , e ) w_{0}\coloneqq w(K_{3},e) where e e is one of the edges of K 3 K_{3} . Then w 0 = ( q ⁡ ( q − 1 ) q ​ ( q − 1 ) 2 ) w_{0}=\binom{q(q-1)}{q(q-1)^{2}} and r ⁡ ( w 0 ) = 1 / ( q − 1 ) r(w_{0})=1/(q-1) . Moreover, on ratios, the operations B B and D = S 2 D=S^{2} act by f B ​ ( r ) = ( q − 1 ) ​ r r + q − 2 , f D ​ ( r ) = r + q − 2 ( q − 1 ) 2 . f_{B}(r)=\frac{(q-1)r}{r+q-2},\qquad f_{D}(r)=\frac{r+q-2}{(q-1)^{2}}. Let I ≔ ( 1 / q , 1 ) I\coloneqq(1/q,1) . Then f B ​ ( I ) ⊆ ( 1 / ( q − 1 ) , 1 ) f_{B}(I)\subseteq\bigl(1/(q-1),\,1) and f D ​ ( I ) ⊆ ( 1 / q , 1 / ( q − 1 ) ) f_{D}(I)\subseteq\bigl(1/q,\,1/(q-1)\bigr) , so the images are disjoint and contained in I I . Consequently, if w ≠ w ′ w\neq w^{\prime} are words in the semigroup { B , D } ∗ \{B,D\}^{\ast} , then r ⁡ ( 𝐰 ⁡ ( w , K 3 , e ) ) ≠ r ⁡ ( 𝐰 ⁡ ( w ′ , K 3 , e ) ) r(\mathbf{w}(w,K_{3},e))\neq r(\mathbf{w}(w^{\prime},K_{3},e)) . Proof. The base computation is immediate: K 3 / e K_{3}/e is a double edge, so P K 3 / e ​ ( q ) = q ⁡ ( q − 1 ) P_{K_{3}/e}(q)=q(q-1) ; K 3 − e K_{3}-e is the path on three vertices, so P K 3 − e ​ ( q ) = q ​ ( q − 1 ) 2 P_{K_{3}-e}(q)=q(q-1)^{2} . Notice that r ⁡ ( w 0 ) ∈ I r(w_{0})\in I . From Lemma 4.3 , if w = ( x y ) w=\binom{x}{y} then B ​ w = ( ( q − 1 ) ​ x x + ( q − 2 ) ​ y ) , D ​ w = ( x + ( q − 2 ) ​ y ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ y ) , Bw=\binom{(q-1)x}{x+(q-2)y},\qquad Dw=\binom{x+(q-2)y}{(q-1)^{2}y}, so the ratio maps are f B , f D f_{B},f_{D} . Now fix r ∈ I r\in I , so 1 / q < r < 1 1/q<r<1 . Then, since q > 2 q>2 , f B f_{B} is strictly increasing, so f B ​ ( r ) > f B ​ ( 1 / q ) = 1 q − 1 and f B ​ ( r ) < f B ​ ( 1 ) = 1 . f_{B}(r)>f_{B}(1/q)=\frac{1}{q-1}\quad\text{and}\quad f_{B}(r)<f_{B}(1)=1. Thus f B ​ ( I ) ⊆ ( 1 / ( q − 1 ) , 1 ) f_{B}(I)\subseteq(1/(q-1),\,1) . Similarly, since f D f_{D} is strictly increasing, f D ​ ( r ) > f D ​ ( 1 / q ) = 1 q and f D ​ ( r ) < f D ​ ( 1 ) = 1 q − 1 , f_{D}(r)>f_{D}(1/q)=\frac{1}{q}\quad\text{and}\quad f_{D}(r)<f_{D}(1)=\frac{1}{q-1}, so f D ​ ( I ) ⊆ ( 1 / q , 1 / ( q − 1 ) ) f_{D}(I)\subseteq(1/q,\,1/(q-1)) . Hence, the images are disjoint. Finally, suppose r ⁡ ( 𝐰 ⁡ ( w , K 3 , e ) ) = r ⁡ ( 𝐰 ⁡ ( w ′ , K 3 , e ) ) r(\mathbf{w}(w,K_{3},e))=r(\mathbf{w}(w^{\prime},K_{3},e)) . Then their ratios agree and lie in I I . If at least one of w , w ′ w,w^{\prime} is empty, say w ′ w^{\prime} , then r ⁡ ( 𝐰 ⁡ ( w , K 3 , e ) ) = r ⁡ ( w ⁡ ( K 3 , e ) ) = 1 / ( q − 1 ) ∉ f B ​ ( I ) ∪ f D ​ ( I ) r(\mathbf{w}(w,K_{3},e))=r(w(K_{3},e))=1/(q-1)\not\in f_{B}(I)\cup f_{D}(I) . Hence, w w must also be empty. If both w , w ′ w,w^{\prime} are non-empty, by the image disjointness just proved, the last letter of 