 the definition), but using higher powers of S S and concatenations of S S and B B . We begin with the following lemma, which gives the ratio action of a block operation of higher powers of S S . Lemma 5.2 . For m ≥ 1 m\geq 1 and q ≠ 1 q\neq 1 , we have (9) ( r ∘ w ∘ S 2 ​ m ) ​ ( G , e ) = 1 q + ( r ∘ w ) ​ ( G , e ) − 1 q ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ m . (r\circ w\circ S^{2m})(G,e)=\frac{1}{q}+\frac{(r\circ w)(G,e)-\frac{1}{q}}{(q-1)^{2m}}. Proof. From the computation in Lemma 4.5 we see that ( r ∘ S 2 ) ​ ( u ) = r ⁡ ( u ) + q − 2 ( q − 1 ) 2 (r\circ S^{2})(u)=\frac{r(u)+q-2}{(q-1)^{2}} for every attainable vector u u . In particular, when u = ( w ∘ S 2 ​ i ) ​ ( G , e ) u=(w\circ S^{2i})(G,e) , i ∈ { 0 , 1 , … , m − 1 } i\in\{0,1,\dots,m-1\} , one obtains ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ i + 2 ​ ( r ∘ w ∘ S 2 ​ i + 2 ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ i ​ ( r ∘ w ∘ S 2 ​ i ) ​ ( G , e ) + ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ i ​ ( q − 2 ) . (q-1)^{2i+2}(r\circ w\circ S^{2i+2})(G,e)=(q-1)^{2i}(r\circ w\circ S^{2i})(G,e)+(q-1)^{2i}(q-2). These equations telescope into ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ m ​ ( r ∘ w ∘ S 2 ​ m ) ​ ( G , e ) = ( r ∘ w ) ​ ( G , e ) + ( q − 2 ) ​ ( q − 1 ) 2 ​ m − 1 ( q − 1 ) 2 − 1 , (q-1)^{2m}(r\circ w\circ S^{2m})(G,e)=(r\circ w)(G,e)+(q-2)\frac{(q-1)^{2m}-1}{(q-1)^{2}-1}, which implies ( 9 ). ∎ Using these block operations of higher powers of S S , we can prove the following ping–pong lemma. Lemma 5.3 . Let q ∈ ( 0 , 2 ) ∖ { 1 } q\in(0,2)\setminus\{1\} . Then, there exists a planar graph G 0 G_{0} , an edge e 0 e_{0} of G 0 G_{0} and block operations K = K ⁡ ( q ) K=K(q) and L = L ⁡ ( q ) L=L(q) that are each a sequence of operations in { B , S } \{B,S\} such that the following is true. If, for every word a = ( a 1 , … , a t ) a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{t}) in the semigroup { K , L } ∗ \{K,L\}^{\ast} , we let 𝐰 ~ ​ ( a ) ≔ ( w ∘ a t ∘ a t − 1 ∘ ⋯ ∘ a 1 ) ​ ( G 0 , e 0 ) , \widetilde{\mathbf{w}}(a)\coloneqq(w\circ a_{t}\circ a_{t-1}\circ\dots\circ a_{1})(G_{0},e_{0}), the attainable vector witnessed by the pair generated th