xp ⁡ ( ( c ⁡ ( q ) + o ⁡ ( 1 ) ) ​ n ) \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq\exp\left((c(q)+o(1))n\right) . However, a more detailed analysis easily can show that we may choose c ⁡ ( q ) c(q) to be an absolute constant, namely 1 / 8 1/8 . • For q ∈ ( 0 , 1.458 ) q\in(0,1.458) , the construction in Case 1 of Lemma 5.3 works with m = 1 m=1 . This construction provides the bound | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 n / 4 − o ⁡ ( n ) \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/4-o(n)} ; • For q ∈ ( 1 , 3 / 2 ] q\in(1,3/2] , one can use the construction in Case 3 of Lemma 5.3 . This construction provides the bound | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 n / 6 − o ⁡ ( n ) \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)} ; • For q ∈ ( 3 / 2 , 1.53 ) q\in(3/2,1.53) , one can slightly modify the construction in Case 3 of Lemma 5.3 , picking ( G 0 , e 0 , K , L , I , J ) = ( K 4 , e , S 4 , B ∘ S 2 ∘ B , ( − ∞ , − 0.5 ] , ( − 0.5 , 0.56 ] ) . (G_{0},e_{0},K,L,I,J)=\big(K_{4},e,S^{4},B\circ S^{2}\circ B,(-\infty,-0.5],(-0.5,0.56]\big). This choice provides the bound | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 n / 8 − o ⁡ ( n ) \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/8-o(n)} ; • For q ∈ [ 1.53 , 2 ] q\in[1.53,2] , the construction in Case 2 of Lemma 5.3 works with m = 2 m=2 . This construction provides the bound | 𝖲 n pl ​ ( q ) | ≥ 2 n / 6 − o ⁡ ( n ) \left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)} . We leave the details to the interested reader. Notice that Theorems 2.12 , 4.1 and 5.1 , under the observations of Remark 5.4 , imply Theorem 1.1 . 6. Concluding remarks Superexponential spectra. For every fixed real q ∉ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\notin\{0,1,2\} we proved that the evaluation spectrum 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) = { P G ​ ( q ) : | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n } \mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;=\;\{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\} grows exponentially in n n , with explicit lower bounds of different qualities in various regimes. For positive integers q ∉ { 0 , 1 , 2 } q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\} , we also noted that exponential growth for the evaluation spectrum of P G ​ ( q ) P_{G}(q) is best possible (up to the correct base of the exponent), due to the fact that | 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) | ≤ q n + 1 |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1 . Nevertheless, at other values of q q , the size of 𝖲 n ​ ( q ) \mathsf{S}_{n}(q) could be significantly larger, in principle. For example, when q = − 1 q=-1 , | P G ​ ( − 1 ) | |P_{G}(-1)| counts the number of acyclic orientations of G G , so the natural upper bound for | 𝖲 n ​ ( − 1 ) | |\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)| is | P K n ​ ( − 1 ) | = n ! |P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n! , rather than q n + 1 q^{n}+1 . In this direction, we would like to make the appealing following conjecture for the spectrum of acyclic orientations. Conjecture 6.1 . | 𝖲 n ​ ( − 1 ) | ​ is superexponential in ​ n . |\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)|\ \text{is superexponential in\ }n. A closely related “spectrum” question concerns the unrestrained spanning–tree spectrum 𝒯 ⁡ ( n ) \mathcal{T}(n) . We discussed the recent works of Chan–Kontorovich–Pak and of Alon–Bucić–Gishboliner [ 8 , 3 ] which show that | 𝒯 pl ​ ( n ) | |\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)| is exponential in n n , if one restricts the count to planar graphs. In the unrestricted setting, however, the extremal values are also superexponential (recall Cayley’s theorem that τ ⁡ ( K n ) = n n − 2 \tau(K_{n})=n^{n-2} ), so it is a natural to conjecture that | 𝒯 ⁡ ( n ) | |\mathcal{T}(n)| is also superexponential. In this direction, we would like to reiterate the very appealing [ 8 , Conjecture 5.2] . Conjecture 6.2 . | 𝒯 ⁡ ( n ) | = e Ω ⁡ ( n ​ log ⁡ n ) . |\mathcal{T}(n)|=e^{\Omega(n\log n)}. How far does the ping–pong method go? Our arguments use remarkably little about chromatic polynomials beyond the existence of a two–term deletion–contraction relation and the ability to realize local operations as linear maps on a small “boundary” space. This suggests that similar results should be possible for a broader class of Tutte–type invariants. For example, the Tutte polynomial T G ​ ( x , y ) T_{G}(x,y) (and equivalently the Potts/random–cluster partition function) is universal among Tutte–Grothendieck invariants and is governed by deletion–contraction [ 13 ] . It would be interesting to study evaluation spectra such as { T G ​ ( x 0 , y 0 ) : | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n } or { Z G ​ ( q 0 , v 0 ) : | V ⁡ ( G ) | = n } \{\,T_{G}(x_{0},y_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\{\,Z_{G}(q_{0},v_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\} at fixed parameters, and to understand when the same feasible–vector/ping–pong mechanism yields exponential (or even superexponential) growth. Here Z G ​ ( q , v ) Z_{G}(q,v) denotes the random–cluster (Potts) partition function, a standard specialization of the Tutte polynomial. In particular, it would be interesting to determine the specializations of the Tutte polynomial (or more general Tutte–Grothendieck invariants) that guarantee exponential growth of the corresponding evaluation spectrum, as well as the exceptional locus where the spectrum is subexponential. References [1] I. Agol and V. Krushkal, Tutte relations, TQFT, and planarity of cubic graphs , Illinois Journal of Mathematics, Volume 60, Number 1, Spring 2016, Pages 273–288. [2] I. Agol and V. S. Krushkal, Structure of the flow and Yamada polynomials of cubic graphs , Breadth in contemporary topology , 1–20, Proc. Sympos. Pure Math., 102, Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, RI, 2019. [3] N. Alon, M. Bucić, and L. Gishboliner, The spanning tree spectrum: improved bounds and simple proofs , Proceedings of the AMS, to appear. [4] M. Beck and T. Zaslavsky, Inside-out polytopes , Adv. Math. 205 (2006), no. 1, 134–162. [5] G. D. Birkhoff, A determinant formula for the number of ways of coloring a map , Ann. of Math. (2) 14 (1912), 42–46. [6] J. Bourgain and A. Kontorovich, On Zaremba’s conjecture , Ann. of Math. (2) 180 (2014), no. 1, 137–196. [7] J. L. Brenner and A. Charnow, Free semigroups of 2 × 2 2\times 2 matrices , Pacific J. Math. 77 (1978), no. 1, 57–69. [8] S. H. Chan, A. Kontorovich, and I. Pak, Spanning trees and continued fractions , preprint, arXiv:2411.18782 (2024). [9] C. Löh, Geometric group theory, An introduction , Universitext, Springer, Cham, 2017. [10] R. P. Stanley, Acyclic orientations of graphs , Discrete Math. 5 (1973), 171–178. [11] R. P. Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics, Vol. 1 , 2nd ed., Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 49, Cambridge University Press, 2012. [12] D. Treumann, How many chromatic polynomials of planar maps are there? , MathOverflow question 241734 (2016), answer by I. Agol. [13] W. T. Tutte, A contribution to the theory of chromatic polynomials , Canad. J. Math. 6 (1954), 80–91. [14] H. Whitney, Non-separable and planar graphs , Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 34 (1932), no. 2, 339–362. [15] H. Whitney, A logical expansion in mathematics , Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 38 (1932), 572–579. [16] S. K. Zaremba, La méthode des “bons treillis” pour le calcul des intégrales multiples , Applications of number theory to numerical analysis (Proc. Sympos., Univ. Montréal, Montreal, Que., 1971), Academic Press, New York–London, 1972, pp. 39–119. Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the "Report Issue" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all. Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collabor